module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.Decent.VeryReasonablePermanence
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.Representable.BaseChange
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Thickenings.BaseChange
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.HTopology.AlmostBlowUpSheafThickening
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.HTopology.EqualizerTransport
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.HTopology.SheafCriterion

/-!
# Replacing the base by a thickening

In the sufficiency direction of Stacks 0EVF one is given a morphism `f : X ⟶ Y` of the big h
category over `S` and wishes to verify the sheaf condition for the one-element family `{f}`.
At one point in the argument the base `Y` is replaced by a thickening `u : Y₀ ⟶ Y` of finite
presentation and `X` by the base change `X₀ = X ×_Y Y₀`: the source says "by the Interlude B we
have `F(Y₀) = F(Y)`, `F(X₀) = F(X)` and `F(X₀ ×_{Y₀} X₀) = F(X ×_Y X)`", so that "we may replace
`Y` by `Y₀` and `X` by `X₀`".

This file carries out that replacement.  The interlude it appeals to is Interlude A of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.HTopology.AlmostBlowUpSheafThickening`, namely
`AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.bijective_map_of_isThickening`: a presheaf satisfying conditions (1)
and (3) of Stacks 0EVF sends a thickening of finite presentation of the site to a bijection.  The
three identifications the source quotes are the three instances of it at `u`, at `v : X₀ ⟶ X` and
at the induced morphism `X₀ ×_{Y₀} X₀ ⟶ X ×_Y X`, and the sheaf condition is moved across them by
the equalizer transport of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.HTopology.EqualizerTransport`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.isSheafFor_singleton_of_isSheafFor_singleton_of_isThickening`: the
  replacement step — given a cartesian square of the site whose bottom row is a thickening of
  finite presentation, the sheaf condition for the one-element family generated by the top row
  carries the sheaf condition for the one-element family generated by the bottom row.

## Implementation notes

Both sheaf conditions are turned into bijectivity of the canonical map to an equalizer by
`AlgebraicGeometry.isSheafFor_singleton_iff_bijective_mapToEqualizer`, with the chosen fibre
products `X ×_Y X` and `X₀ ×_{Y₀} X₀` of the *site* as the common refinements.  Working in the
site rather than in schemes is what keeps the proof short: the comparison morphism is then the
canonical `CategoryTheory.Limits.pullback.map`, and no pullback comparison isomorphism has to be
threaded through the argument.

The hypothesis is that the square is cartesian on *underlying* schemes, but it is then already
cartesian in the site: the inclusion of the site into schemes preserves fibre products and
reflects isomorphisms, hence reflects them, so `CategoryTheory.IsPullback.of_map` applies.  With
the square available in the site, that the comparison `q : X₀ ×_{Y₀} X₀ ⟶ X ×_Y X` is again a
base change of `u` — the identification `X₀ ×_{Y₀} X₀ = (X ×_Y X) ×_Y Y₀` — is
`CategoryTheory.IsPullback.pullbackFst`, which makes `q` a base change of `v`, pasted vertically
with the given square by `CategoryTheory.IsPullback.paste_vert`; the underlying square of schemes
is its image under `CategoryTheory.Functor.map_isPullback`.

That the two base changes `v` and `q` of `u` are again thickenings of finite presentation is
`Stacks.Thickenings.isThickening_of_isPullback` for the thickening half (Stacks 09ZU) and
`CategoryTheory.MorphismProperty.of_isPullback` against the instance
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.finitePresentation_isStableUnderBaseChange` (Stacks 02WF) for finite
presentation.

